WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.032
Now we put the classification workflow together.

00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:10.817
We create X and y, split the data, create a Logistic Regression model, fit it on the training rows,

00:00:10.817 --> 00:00:12.942
and then predict the test rows.

00:00:12.942 --> 00:00:17.065
Run the code and compare predicted labels with the actual labels.

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:22.159
Remember that the model has not seen the test targets during training.

00:00:22.159 --> 00:00:27.659
That is why the comparison is meaningful.

00:00:27.659 --> 00:00:35.119
If we only evaluated the model on its training data, we would be measuring how well it fits known examples rather than

00:00:35.119 --> 00:00:42.874
how well it generalizes. This train-fit-predict pattern appears across many supervised algorithms.

00:00:42.874 --> 00:00:50.309
Next semester, you may replace Logistic Regression with a decision tree, random forest, support vector machine,

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:55.612
or another classifier, but the surrounding workflow will look very familiar.
